Direct taxes generally attach to income or wealth; indirect taxes attach to specified transactions or consumption.|Income tax and a consumption tax have different bases and collection mechanics.|Every tax uses the same taxable base?|No|Yes|Only nationally
Taxable income follows tax law; accounting income follows reporting rules, creating permanent and temporary differences.|Book depreciation differs from allowable tax depreciation.|Accounting profit necessarily equals taxable profit?|No|Yes|Only for corporations
Marginal rates apply to the next relevant unit; effective rates compare total tax with a defined income base.|Tax ₹20 on income ₹100 gives effective 20%, even if the next unit faces 30%.|Rate relevant to the next unit is…?|Marginal rate|Average effective rate always|Gross margin
Individual and corporate taxes differ by legal taxpayer, residence, income type, and period.|A company distributes profits to an owner under jurisdiction-specific rules.|Company and owner always face identical tax treatment?|No|Yes|Only for private firms
Gains, dividends, and interest can have different tax rates, timing, exemptions, and withholding rules.|Selling an investment can trigger a gain distinct from periodic interest.|One universal rate applies to all investment income?|No|Yes|Only for small accounts
Product taxation depends on legal structure, holding period, account, and investor status.|Two investments with equal gross returns can yield different net outcomes.|Compare only advertised pre-tax returns?|No|Yes|Only for funds
Tax depreciation and capital allowances determine deduction timing independently of book charges.|Accelerated deductions reduce current taxable income under assumed eligibility.|A timing benefit is necessarily a permanent exemption?|No|Yes|Only for equipment
Loss use depends on allowed offsets, expiry, ownership changes, and other restrictions.|A ₹10 eligible loss offsets ₹10 future taxable income under stated hypothetical rules.|Every loss is immediately refundable in cash?|No|Yes|Only for companies
Deferred tax recognises specified future tax effects of temporary differences under accounting standards.|Book carrying value exceeds tax base, potentially creating a liability.|Deferred tax directly states tomorrow's cash payment?|No|Yes|Only at year-end
GST can create output obligations and eligible input credits; payment and recovery timing affect cash.|A firm pays tax on purchases before using an eligible credit.|This can temporarily increase…?|Working-capital needs|Gross revenue automatically|Equity capital
Withholding collects tax at source; credit or final-tax treatment depends on relevant rules.|A payer withholds ₹10 from a ₹100 gross payment: cash receipt ₹90.|₹90 cash necessarily means recognised gross income ₹90?|No|Yes|Only for interest
Cross-border tax considers residence, source, treaties, withholding, and relief mechanisms.|A treaty benefit may require eligibility and documentation.|Treaties automatically eliminate all tax?|No|Yes|Only for dividends
Tax transfer pricing assesses related-party terms under applicable rules, often using arm's-length principles.|A group sells goods internally at an unusually low price.|Tax review focuses on…?|Defensible terms and documentation|Only consolidated revenue|Only internal preference
Transaction taxation depends on assets or shares, jurisdiction, structure, losses, and financing.|Two economically similar deals can generate different tax cash flows.|Legal structure can affect after-tax value?|Yes|No|Only after closing
Tax planning uses lawful choices; evasion involves unlawful concealment or misrepresentation under applicable law.|Fabricated deductions differ from a legitimate eligible allowance.|Inventing expenses is lawful planning?|No|Yes|Only if unnoticed
After-tax valuation models taxes consistently with cash, financing, and investor perspective.|A ₹10 taxable return at assumed 20% tax leaves ₹8 before other costs.|After-tax amount is…?|₹8|₹10|₹12
